   "read_on": "2026-10-26",
   "how_read": "Public Gallup PDFs, saved and converted with pdftotext -layout, then parsed by a script and checked. Totals: the Q44 trend table of the March 2026 topline (1956-2026, 53 readings), printed 'Too high' percentages; the 2016, 2017, 2019, 2020, 2021, 2023 and 2025 toplines print identical figures for every year they share (checked). Gallup's separate 1943-1953 'earlier trend (asked of taxpayers)' is a different universe and is left out. The item sat in the April Economy and Personal Finance survey through 2024 and moved to the March survey in 2025. Cells: the 'Public Release Data' crosstab of the same question in the 2019, 2020, 2021, 2023, 2025 and 2026 toplines, printed percentages; each crosstab's Total equals the trend figure (checked). No topline PDF was found for 2018, 2022 or 2024, and the 2016-2017 toplines carry no crosstab. Only Male, Female, Some College and White are kept as cells. 'White' (Gallup's Race I banner) is mapped to race_eth=white_nh by inference: these PDFs do not define Race I, but the 2025 June Confidence topline prints the White crosstab group's figures under 'Non-Hispanic White adults' (https://news.gallup.com/file/poll/692660/2025_07_17_Confidence%20in%20Institutions%20Topline%20and%20Tabs.pdf). 'published' is the date of the Gallup article that first reports the figure (read on the article page), null where none was found or read. No opinion is not rescaled."
  }
